# Assessment Activity

## Phase 1 — Public Application Reconnaissance

Public application responses were collected outside the browser for
analysis\.

```bash
curl -sL https://TARGET/login -o login.html
```

Returned application content was searched for authentication and
API\-related references:

```bash
grep -Eio '/api/[^" ]+|/auth/[^" ]+' login.html | sort -u
```

This provided an initial view of routes and application components
exposed to an unauthenticated client\.

### Result

The application exposed sufficient client\-side information to identify
its general framework and authentication architecture\. No vulnerability
was assigned solely on the basis of technology or route disclosure\.

---

## Phase 2 — Authentication Flow Analysis

The login process was inspected through the browser’s Network panel\.

Authentication submissions generated requests containing the expected
email and password fields\.

Sanitized request structure:

```json
{
  "email": "test@example.com",
  "password": "<redacted>"
}
```

Invalid credentials resulted in an HTTP `400` response containing an
authentication error equivalent to:

```json
{
  "code": "invalid_credentials",
  "message": "Invalid login credentials"
}
```

Additional invalid test credentials produced the same general response
behavior\.

### Assessment Result

No authentication bypass was identified during this phase\.

A password\-recovery workflow was not implemented in the tested version
of the application and was therefore excluded from the current
authentication testing\.

---

## Phase 3 — Administrative Route Discovery

Reconnaissance identified the following administrative route:

```text
/admin
```

Direct browser navigation to this location did not display the
administrative interface\. Instead, the application presented its
authentication page while retaining the requested administrative
destination:

```text
/login?next=/admin
```

The route was then examined independently from the browser\.

```bash
curl -sS -i https://TARGET/admin
```

The returned application content was saved for additional analysis:

```bash
curl -s https://TARGET/admin -o /tmp/admin.html
```

Relevant terms were extracted from the response:

```bash
grep -Eio 'dashboard|users|orders|payments|admin|login|sign.?in' /tmp/admin.html | sort -u
```

Observed terms included references to the administrative and login
routes\.

---

## Phase 4 — Unauthenticated Administrative Access Validation

The `/admin` response required additional analysis because receiving
application content alone did not establish whether protected
functionality was exposed\.

Inspection of the returned Next\.js data identified redirect behavior
containing:

```text
NEXT_REDIRECT
/login?next=/admin
307
```

This demonstrated that an unauthenticated request was being routed into
the authentication workflow rather than being granted access to the
protected administrative interface\.

### Validation Result

Control Tested                                               Result

---

Administrative route externally discoverable                 Yes
`/admin` reachable without an existing session               Yes
Administrative interface accessible without authentication   No
Unauthenticated request redirected to login                  Yes
Administrative authentication bypass confirmed               No

### Security Disposition

**No finding issued\.**

The administrative route was discoverable, but testing did not
demonstrate unauthorized access to administrative functionality or
protected content\.

